%S A399897 2,4,8,17,30,44,75,86,116,133,145,204,208,271,294,322,345,364,368,404,
%T A399897 426,482,506,557,602,853,939,979,1059,1109,1117,1124,1148,1219,1284,
%U A399897 1309,1368,1511,1585,1872,2032,2134,2193,2324,2398,2549,2729,2877,2906,3050
%N A399897 First elements of the rows of the grasshopper infinite barrycade (recursively defined family of permutations of the positive integers having pairwise disjoint sets of partial sums).
%C A399897 A barrycade is a collection of rows whose sets of partial sums are pairwise disjoint. In the infinite version considered here, each row is a permutation of the positive integers.
%C A399897 The grasshopper barrycade is constructed row by row. Suppose some initial part of the current row has already been chosen and its sum is s. Let k be the smallest positive integer not yet used in that row. The construction chooses the smallest positive integer k' not yet used in the row, with k' != k, such that neither s+k' nor s+k'+k is a partial sum occurring in any earlier row. It then appends k', followed by k, and repeats this procedure indefinitely. In this way every row becomes a permutation of the positive integers, while partial sums belonging to different rows never coincide. a(n) is the first element of the n-th row.
%C A399897 Unlike the precise grasshopper barrycade A399900, this construction does not necessarily cover every positive integer by a partial sum. Some positive integers are missed.
%C A399897 The spelling "barrycade" is due to Richard Guy, who used it instead of "barricade" in honor of Barry Cipra, who prompted him to study the problem.
%H A399897 Sean A. Irvine, <a href="/A399897/b399897.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..500</a>
%H A399897 M. Dębski, J. Grytczuk, P. Naroski, B. Pawlik, J. Przybyło and M. Śleszyńska-Nowak, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.18476">Finite and infinite barrycades</a>, arXiv:2609.18476 [math.CO], 2026.
%F A399897 a(n) is the first term of the n-th row generated by the grasshopper construction described in the Comments.
%F A399897 a(n) = min { m >= 1 : A399899(m)=n }.
%e A399897 The first four rows begin
%e A399897 psi_1 = ( 2, 1, 4, 3,  6, 5, 8, 7, 10, 9, ...),
%e A399897 psi_2 = ( 4, 1, 6, 2,  7, 3, 9, 5, 11, 8, ...),
%e A399897 psi_3 = ( 8, 1, 3, 2, 10, 4, 6, 5, 11, 7, ...),
%e A399897 psi_4 = (17, 1, 7, 2,  8, 3, 5, 4, 11, 6, ...).
%e A399897 Hence a(1)=2, a(2)=4, a(3)=8, and a(4)=17.
%t A399897 A399897[n_] := Module[{u, s, p, init, step, extend, covered},covered[0, _] = False;covered[r_, x_] := AnyTrue[Range[r],(init[#]; extend[#, x]; MemberQ[p[#], x]) &];step[r_] := Module[{k = 1, q = 1},While[MemberQ[u[r], k], k++];While[MemberQ[u[r], q] || q == k || covered[r - 1, s[r] + q] || covered[r - 1, s[r] + q + k], q++]; Scan[(AppendTo[u[r], #]; s[r] += #; AppendTo[p[r], s[r]]) &, {q, k}]]; init[r_] := If[!ValueQ[s[r]], u[r] = {}; s[r] = 0; p[r] = {}; step[r]]; extend[r_, x_] := (init[r]; While[s[r] < x, step[r]]);
%t A399897 Table[init[r]; First[u[r]], {r, n}]]
%Y A399897 Cf. A399898-A399909 for other sequences arising from infinite barrycade constructions.
%K A399897 nonn,nice,new
%O A399897 1,1
